      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137517,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases are located on the lower lid margin. Smaller numbers of cases are seen on the upper lid or canthal area {29053036;26265653}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137518,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The duration of the lesion before presentation ranges from 2&nbsp;months to 70&nbsp;years (mean: 24&nbsp;years). The lesion size range is 4&ndash;25&nbsp;mm. The main symptoms are swelling, pruritis, and bleeding. The appearance can be nodular, papular, verrucous, cystic, ulcerated, or crusted, or an umbilicated pore that oozes fluid {29053036;26265653}. SCAP can be misdiagnosed as basal cell carcinoma, sebaceous cyst, recurrent chalazion, and hidrocystoma {29053036;26265653}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137519,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The age range is 8&ndash;82&nbsp;years (mean: 44&nbsp;years). The M:F&nbsp;ratio is 1:2, and the lesion mostly affects White ethnic groups {29053036;26265653}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137520,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137521,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Activation of mutations in the MAPK signalling pathway, affecting <em>BRAF</em>, <em>KRAS</em>, and <em>HRAS</em>, are present in SCAP and tubular adenoma {25532942;29241739;35158743}. These activating mutations increase cell growth, differentiation, and survival.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137522,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 137523,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 60,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SCAP is an endophytic crateriform lesion, connected focally to the epidermis&nbsp;/ hair follicle and composed of two layers of cells forming dilated papillary and cystic structures. The luminal cells are columnar with rounded or oval nuclei near the basal pole of the cell and abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm with decapitation secretion. Mucinous metaplasia may be observed. The outer layer consists of basal/myoepithelial cells. The overlying squamous epithelium often displays prominent hyperplasia. The epidermis surrounding SCAP is often papillomatous or wart like, particularly in cases associated with naevus sebaceus of Jadassohn. The stroma contains numerous plasma cells.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>BRAF p.V600E (antibody clone VE1) can be assessed by immunohistochemistry {29206289} and is seen in SCAP and tubular adenoma. The luminal cells of SCAP and tubular adenoma are positive for CEA and EMA, whereas the outer cell layer is typically reactive for myoepithelial markers, including SMA, calponin, SOX10, and p63 [[Requena L, Sangüeza O. Cutaneous Adnexal Neoplasms. Cham (Switzerland): Springer; 2017. pp. 12–38.]]. A focal lack of myoepithelial cells can be seen in areas with prominent squamous metaplasia or intraluminal secretions.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>The histopathological differential diagnosis of SCAP includes hidradenoma papilliferum {26863059}. Hidradenoma papilliferum has papillary formations similar to those of SCAP, and the epithelial cells lining these structures also show evidence of secretion by decapitation. However, hidradenoma papilliferum is not connected to the epidermis and lacks plasma cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
